.\" ctron(1) manual page. Keep in step with `ctron --help` and
.\" src/cmds.c; plain ASCII on purpose (groff escapes for the few
.\" symbols), so every groff/mandoc build renders it without warnings.
.TH CTRON 1 "" "ctron" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ctron \- ASUS laptop control center: fullscreen TUI and headless CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ctron
.RB [ \-\-tui ]
.br
.B ctron
.I flag
.RI [ flag ...]
.br
.B ctron mode list
|
.BI "mode show" " name"
|
.BI "mode add" " name " steps
|
.BI "mode delete" " name"
.br
.B ctron snapshot
.RB ( alias:
.BR profile )
.I list
|
.BI "apply" " name"
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
|
.BI "diff" " name"
|
.BI "export" " name"
|
.BI "delete" " name"
.br
.B ctron fancurve
.RB ( alias:
.BR fan\-curve )
.I list
|
.BI "apply" " name"
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
|
.BI "diff" " name"
|
.BI "export" " name"
|
.BI "delete" " name"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ctron
controls fans, power limits, CPU frequency ceilings, the battery charge
limit, display refresh rate and keyboard lighting of ASUS (TUF) laptops,
and shows their telemetry.
.PP
Without arguments it opens a fullscreen terminal user interface (a
terminal is required; without one it prints the help instead and exits
2). With any argument it runs headless: it performs the requested
action, prints the result and exits, so it is safe in scripts and over
ssh.
.PP
Every write is an explicit user action: nothing is written when the
program starts or while the TUI polls. A write goes through one chain:
.B asusctl
when it is installed (no root needed), else a direct sysfs write, else
.BR "sudo \-n" ,
passwordless sudo only. Without a usable door the action fails cleanly;
you never get a password prompt or a hang. Writes are verified by reading
the value back. Values that cannot be read reliably are shown as
\fB\-\-\fP, never as a guess.
.PP
On a locked\-PPT laptop the CPU watt limits are volatile: they clear on
reboot, suspend and profile changes. Within the same boot
.B ctron
remembers what it applied and shows it as \fB(set)\fP; a snapshot can be
re\-applied automatically with a Boot snapshot (see
.BR \-\-apply\-startup ).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global modifiers
These are recognised anywhere on the command line and removed before
anything else is parsed.
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-dir " DIR"
Use \fIDIR\fP as the configuration directory instead of
\fB$CTRON_CONFIG\fP, \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ctron\fP or \fB~/.config/ctron\fP.
.BI \-\-config\-dir= DIR
is accepted too.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output for \fB\-\-status\fP and \fB\-\-doctor\fP only;
unknown values are emitted as JSON \fBnull\fP.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Validate the flags, mode, snapshot or fan curve and print what would be
written, without touching hardware.
.SS Information
.TP
.BR \-\-status ", " \-s
Print a live snapshot of every value ctron can read.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch ", " \-w
Print one line of rolling telemetry at a time until interrupted
(Ctrl\-C).
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " file"
Append telemetry rows to \fIfile\fP until interrupted (Ctrl\-C). The
header line is written when the file is new; an existing file must
already have a header with the same columns.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Print a capability report: what each feature will use and whether the
required tool, sysfs node or sudo rule is available.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-tui ", " \-t
Open the TUI explicitly (same as no arguments).
.TP
.B \-\-setup
Print where the configuration lives and exit. An early\-day flag kept
for older instructions; it changes nothing.
.SS Modes, snapshots and fan curves
A \fImode\fP is a named bundle of \fIsteps\fP stored in \fBmodes.ini\fP.
A \fIsnapshot\fP (\fBprofiles/*.ctr\fP) records hardware settings; a
\fIsaved fan curve\fP (\fBfan_curves/*.fc\fP) records one or both fan
curves. A single \fB/\fP places the entry in a group
(\fBgaming/quiet\fP). Snapshot and fan\-curve names keep letters,
digits, \fB.\fP, \fB_\fP and \fB/\fP; any other character becomes
\fB\-\fP, so \(dqmy game\(dq is saved as \fBmy\-game\fP, and \fB.\fP or
\fB..\fP is refused as a group. Mode names may not contain \fB[\fP,
\fB]\fP or a line break.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " name"
Apply the mode \fIname\fP. With \fB\-\-dry\-run\fP validate and print
its steps instead.
.TP
.B \-\-apply\-startup
Apply the Boot snapshot selected in the TUI preferences. It is a no\-op
(exit 0) while that preference is off, which is the default.
.TP
.B mode list
List the modes with their steps.
.TP
.BI "mode show" " name"
Print one mode as it appears in \fBmodes.ini\fP.
.TP
.BI "mode add" " name " steps...
Add a mode. The steps are the remaining arguments joined by spaces, in
the same \fIkey value\fP syntax as a mode file, so quote them:
.RS
.nf
ctron mode add silent \(dqprofile quiet, ppt Q45, fan silent, hz 60\(dq
.fi
.RE
.TP
.BI "mode delete" " name"
Delete a mode.
.TP
.B snapshot list
List the snapshots by name. Also spelled \fBprofile list\fP.
.TP
.BI "snapshot apply" " name"
Apply a snapshot. Fields the snapshot leaves out are not touched. With
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP validate and print the steps instead.
.TP
.BI "snapshot diff" " name"
Show how the snapshot differs from the live hardware.
.TP
.BI "snapshot export" " name"
Save the live hardware state as the snapshot \fIname\fP. An existing
snapshot keeps its note.
.TP
.BI "snapshot delete" " name"
Delete a snapshot.
.PP
\fBfancurve\fP (alias \fBfan\-curve\fP) takes the same five subcommands
for saved fan curves: \fBlist\fP, \fBapply\fP \fIname\fP
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fP], \fBdiff\fP \fIname\fP, \fBexport\fP \fIname\fP and
\fBdelete\fP \fIname\fP.
.SS Hardware flags
Any number of these may be given on one command line; they are applied
in order. The same \fIkey value\fP pairs are the steps of a mode and the
lines of a snapshot file. A flag that fails does not stop the remaining
ones, but the exit status becomes non\-zero. Every flag also accepts the
form \fB\-\-flag=\fP\fIvalue\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " quiet|balanced|performance"
Platform profile (the firmware power mode).
.TP
.BI \-\-ac\-profile " off|quiet|balanced|performance"
Which profile asusd enforces automatically while on AC power.
\fBoff\fP leaves the choice to you; the currently active profile is not
changed.
.TP
.BI \-\-battery\-profile " off|quiet|balanced|performance"
The same for battery power.
.TP
.BI \-\-epp " power|balance_power|balance_performance|performance"
Energy performance preference of the CPU.
.TP
.BI \-\-freq " mhz"
Frequency ceiling for every thread (\fImhz\fP is 100..10000).
.TP
.BI \-\-freq " core " cpu\-id " " mhz
Ceiling for one thread, \fIcpu\-id\fP being the kernel cpu number.
.TP
.BI \-\-freq " ccd " n " " mhz
Ceiling for every thread of CCD \fIn\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-hz " rate|max"
Display refresh rate; the nearest mode the compositor offers is used.
\fBmax\fP selects the highest.
.TP
.BI \-\-battery " 20..100"
Battery charge limit in percent.
.TP
.B \-\-battery\-oneshot
Charge to 100 % once through \fBasusctl battery oneshot\fP. It needs
asusctl, as there is no sysfs path for it.
.TP
.BI \-\-ppt " Q45|B60|P80|spl,sppt,fppt|off|on"
CPU watt limits (SPL, SPPT, FPPT): \fBQ45\fP is 45/55/55 W, \fBB60\fP is
60/75/75 W, \fBP80\fP is 80/80/80 W; or give the three values in watts.
\fBoff\fP lifts the limits to what the BIOS reports as maximum and
remembers the previous values; it is refused when the BIOS does not
report them. \fBon\fP writes the remembered values back; it is a no\-op
(exit 0) when there are none, which the event log says.
.TP
.BI \-\-nv\-boost " 5..25"
NVIDIA dynamic boost in watts.
.TP
.BI \-\-nv\-temp " 75..87"
NVIDIA temperature target in \(deC.
.TP
.BI \-\-gpu\-clock " mhz|reset"
Lock the NVIDIA core clock (\fImhz\fP is 200..4096) or release the lock
(\fBreset\fP; \fBoff\fP is accepted too).
.TP
.BI \-\-panel\-od " on|off"
Panel overdrive.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-boost " on|off"
Global cpufreq boost.
.TP
.BI \-\-kbd " off|low|med|high"
Keyboard backlight brightness.
.TP
.BI \-\-aura " effect " [ color | hex ]
Keyboard lighting. \fIeffect\fP is one of \fBstatic\fP, \fBbreathe\fP,
\fBrainbow\-cycle\fP, \fBrainbow\-wave\fP, \fBpulse\fP, \fBcomet\fP,
\fBflash\fP, \fBstars\fP, \fBrain\fP, \fBhighlight\fP, \fBlaser\fP,
\fBripple\fP, whichever the keyboard offers (\fBctron \-\-doctor\fP
lists them). \fIcolor\fP is \fBIce\fP, \fBRed\fP,
\fBGreen\fP, \fBBlue\fP, \fBYellow\fP, \fBPurple\fP, \fBOrange\fP or
\fBWhite\fP; \fIhex\fP is six hex digits (\fBffa500\fP). An effect the
keyboard does not have is refused without writing.
.TP
.BI \-\-fan " stock|silent|cool|full|on|off"
\fBstock\fP hands both fans back to the EC (factory control);
\fBsilent\fP, \fBcool\fP and \fBfull\fP apply that preset to both fans;
\fBon\fP and \fBoff\fP switch both custom fan curves on or off.
.TP
.BI \-\-fan " cpu|gpu " " on|off"
Switch the custom curve of one fan (two words:
\fB\-\-fan cpu on\fP).
.TP
.BI \-\-fan " curve"
Apply the saved fan curve \fIcurve\fP (\fBctron fancurve list\fP).
.TP
.BI \-\-fan\-curve " cpu|gpu " " temps " " pwms"
Stage a curve for one fan: two comma\-separated lists of up to eight
integers (temperatures in \(deC, PWM 0..255), for example
\fB\-\-fan\-curve cpu 40,60,80 80,140,220\fP. Values are clamped, the
curve is normalised to increasing temperatures and non\-decreasing PWM,
and it stays in memory until \fB\-\-fan\-write\fP.
.TP
.B \-\-fan\-write
Write the staged curve(s) to the EC and verify them by reading back.
.TP
.B \-\-follow
Run the actions bound in \fB~/.config/ctron/daeboard.binds\fP when the
daeboard daemon sends them (runs until stopped).
.TP
.B \-\-daeboard\-start
Start the daeboard daemon through \fBsystemd\-run\fP; needs passwordless
sudo (see SECURITY.md, section 4).
.TP
.B \-\-daeboard\-stop
Ask the running daemon to quit.
.TP
.B \-\-daeboard\-reload
Recompile \fB~/.config/ctron/daeboard.binds\fP.
.PP
\fB\-\-status\fP, \fB\-\-watch\fP, \fB\-\-csv\fP, \fB\-\-doctor\fP,
\fB\-\-mode\fP, \fB\-\-apply\-startup\fP, \fB\-\-follow\fP, the
\fB\-\-daeboard\-...\fP flags and the \fBmode\fP, \fBsnapshot\fP and
\fBfancurve\fP subcommands are stand\-alone commands: they must be the
first argument.
.SH THE TUI
Views are switched with \fB1\fP to \fB5\fP or \fBTab\fP:
\fB1 Power\fP, \fB2 Fans\fP, \fB3 Display & Light\fP, \fB4 Snapshots\fP,
\fB5 Telemetry\fP. Values are staged with \fBj\fP \fBk\fP (row) and
\fBh\fP \fBl\fP (step), or typed exactly with \fBe\fP; \fBw\fP or
\fBEnter\fP applies the staged edits and \fBr\fP reverts them.
\fB,\fP opens the preferences, \fBL\fP the event log, \fB?\fP the keymap
help, \fBEsc\fP backs out of whatever is open and \fBq\fP quits (twice
when edits are pending). The complete keymap is in the project README.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime or verification error: unknown name, rejected value, or a write
that failed or did not read back.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown flag or missing value. Also when ctron is started
without a terminal and without arguments (an explicit \fB\-\-tui\fP
without a terminal exits 1).
.TP
.B 3
A write needed root and did not get it (no passwordless sudo rule, or
the \(dqno sudo\(dq preference is set).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CTRON_CONFIG
Configuration directory (see \fB\-\-config\-dir\fP). Setting it, or
.BR CTRON_TEST ,
also lifts the single\-instance guard of the TUI, so isolated test
instances can run side by side.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the default configuration directory
(\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ctron\fP).
.TP
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ", " TMPDIR
Where the TUI keeps its pidfile (\fBctron\-tui\-<uid>.pid\fP outside
.BR XDG_RUNTIME_DIR ).
.TP
.B DISPLAY_BACKEND
Force a compositor backend for the refresh rate: \fBkde\fP or
\fBhyprland\fP.
.TP
.B DAEBOARD_BIN
Path of the daeboard binary. Default: /usr/local/bin/daeboard, else
~/Documents/daeboard/daeboard.
.TP
.B DAEBOARD_SRC
Directory containing daeboard's \fBinstall.sh\fP, used when the TUI
installs daeboard.
.TP
.B HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE
Read, not set, to detect Hyprland.
.TP
.B TERMINAL
Used by \fBctron\-toggle\fP: the terminal emulator to open the TUI in.
Defaults to \fBkonsole\fP.
.PP
Tools are probed on \fBPATH\fP, never linked: \fBasusctl\fP,
\fBnvidia\-smi\fP, \fBryzenadj\fP, \fBkscreen\-doctor\fP,
\fBhyprctl\fP. A missing one disables its rows; nothing is installed for
you.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ctron/settings.ini
Preferences and remembered hardware state (tagged with the boot id).
.TP
.I ~/.config/ctron/modes.ini
The shortcut modes.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ctron/profiles/*.ctr
Snapshots; subdirectories are groups.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ctron/fan_curves/*.fc
Saved fan curves.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ctron/daeboard.binds
Key binds for the optional daeboard daemon.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ctron-tui.pid
Single\-instance lock of the TUI.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ctron                               # the TUI
ctron \-\-status                     # everything ctron can read, once
ctron \-\-doctor                     # what will work on this machine
ctron \-\-ppt 45,55,65 \-\-dry\-run     # check three watt values, write nothing
ctron \-\-mode turbo                 # a bundle from modes.ini
ctron \-\-gpu\-clock 1400             # lock the GPU core clock
ctron \-\-freq ccd 1 3500            # ceiling for one CCD
ctron \-\-hz 165                      # refresh rate through the compositor
ctron \-\-fan\-curve cpu 40,60,80 80,140,220 \-\-fan\-write
ctron \-\-apply\-startup              # the Boot snapshot, if one is selected
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The project documentation: README.md (keymap, display backends, the M4
key), SECURITY.md (privilege model, sudoers rules, the least\-privilege
\fBctron\-ppt\fP wrapper), HARDWARE.md (what works per machine).
.PP
